      "source": [
        "**Finding:**\n",
        "After changing the surge period to September 19–25, we found that Walmart's average daily orders increased from 575.2 during the baseline period (September 1–10) to 1,361.43 during the surge period. This represents a **136.69% increase in average daily orders**.\n",
        "\n",
        "Looking at the daily orders graph, demand remained relatively consistent during the baseline period, averaging around 575 orders per day. Orders began increasing around September 15, with a much larger increase starting on September 21. Demand peaked around September 24–25, reaching approximately 2,000 orders per day, before dropping afterward.\n",
        "\n",
        "When we compared product categories, we found that **Breakfast Foods had the largest percentage increase at approximately 415%**, followed by Emergency Supplies at approximately 185% and Electronics at approximately 150%. Entertainment, Household, and Health and Wellness also experienced increases of over 100%. All ten product categories showed an increase in average daily units sold during the surge period.\n",
        "\n",
        "We also standardized the product category names before comparing them to avoid counting similar categories separately. Overall, our findings suggest that customers were purchasing more products across multiple categories leading up to landfall, particularly breakfast foods and emergency supplies. However, while demand increased during this period, we cannot conclude that the hurricane directly caused the increase based on transaction data alone."
      ]
    },
