      },
      "source": [
        "**Q3 answer, with its caveat:** We compared daily Walmart orders with historical weather data from 11 Florida store locations to see whether customers increased their shopping before the hurricane. Our results showed that orders started increasing around September 16 and peaked at 1,854 on September 25, before wind speeds increased sharply and atmospheric pressure dropped on September 26. The correlations between daily orders and precipitation (-0.212), wind speed (0.218), and atmospheric pressure (-0.270) were relatively weak, while temperature (0.043) showed almost no relationship. Overall, this suggests that customers may have been stocking up before the storm rather than shopping during the worst weather conditions.\n",
        "\n",
        "Limitation: These correlations only show relationships between weather and orders, not that the hurricane directly caused the increase in sales. We also excluded the Tallahassee store because its coordinates were missing, so our analysis only included 11 of the 12 stores. Additionally, the correlations compare weather and orders on the same day, which may not fully capture customers shopping several days before the storm."
      ]
    },
