      "source": [
        "### Q4 — Stores and Geography\n",
        "\n",
        "We compared Walmart transaction activity across 12 stores during the baseline period (September 1–10, 2024) and the updated surge period (September 19–25, 2024). We cleaned the store IDs by standardizing capitalization, removing extra spaces, and correcting inconsistent hyphens and underscores. This reduced the number of unique store IDs from 60 to 12, ensuring that each store was only counted once. We then calculated the percentage change in average daily orders for each store.\n",
        "\n",
        "Store FL-330 experienced the largest increase in demand, with average daily orders increasing from 45.3 to 115.71, a 155.44% increase. This was followed by FL-302 and FL-156, which both experienced increases of 144.73%. Store FL-239 experienced the smallest increase at 115.43%.\n",
        "\n",
        "All 12 stores experienced an increase in demand during the selected periods, although the percentage increases varied across locations. These findings suggest that Walmart should consider providing additional inventory and staffing to stores experiencing the largest increases in demand during severe weather events. However, because the dataset does not provide specific city or county locations, further geographic analysis would require additional store location information."
      ]
    },
