        "### Q5 — Signal or Artifact\n",
        "\n",
        "We analyzed Walmart's transaction data alongside the sales and inventory database to determine whether the increase in board game demand during the hurricane-related period reflected a real pattern or was influenced by data-quality issues.\n",
        "\n",
        "For this analysis, we used SQLite to access the sales and inventory database and focused specifically on Monopoly board games. We identified three different SKUs associated with Monopoly: BG-MONO, GAME-MONO, and MONOPOLY-01. We combined these SKUs to get a more accurate understanding of total board game sales rather than treating them as separate products.We then compared average daily Monopoly sales during the baseline period (September 1–10) and the surge period (September 19–25). We also examined Entertainment category sales from the SQLite database to see how the broader category performed during the same period. To further investigate whether the increase in demand was supported by the data, we examined inventory levels, including units on hand, units on order, and reorder points. We also checked for duplicate inventory records and compared sales and inventory information by store and date. This helped us identify potential inventory shortages and determine whether inconsistencies in the data could affect our findings.Overall, this analysis allowed us to examine board game demand using both transaction and inventory records rather than relying only on overall Walmart sales. These findings can help Walmart make better inventory decisions, particularly by identifying stores where board game inventory may need to be replenished during periods of increased demand. However, additional validation is needed before concluding that the increase was entirely a genuine demand signal rather than a data artifact"
